84:This paper is organised as follows. Section~\ref{sec:theory} summarises the tSZ power spectrum formalism and fixes the binned covariance. Section~\ref{sec:kernels} shows quantitatively which halos drive each probe and thereby motivates the masking scheme. Section~\ref{sec:masking} develops the masking formalism with intrinsic scatter, including the conditional-moment weighting for the masked power spectrum and trispectrum. Section~\ref{sec:cnc} presents the cluster number count model. Section~\ref{sec:likelihood} describes the joint likelihood, the synthetic data and the halo-painting simulation pipeline. Results appear in Sec.~\ref{sec:results}, and we conclude in Sec.~\ref{sec:conclusions}.
158:\label{sec:kernels}
161:Before introducing the masking formalism, it is useful to ask which halos (in mass and redshift) dominate each observable. This sets the physical expectation for how masking will redistribute information between the two probes. We define the kernels
165:\label{eq:kernels}
167:with $X\in\{C_\ell^{\rm tSZ,\,full},C_\ell^{\rm tSZ,\,unres},N\}$, where $N$ denotes the total resolved cluster count. Each kernel is normalised to unity over the corresponding logarithmic variable, so it can be interpreted as a probability density and used to define the mean contributing redshift $\langle z\rangle$ and the mean contributing mass $\langle M\rangle$.
171:    \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{../../tsz_cnc_paper_plots/kernel_analysis/kernels_all_observables.pdf}
172:    \caption{Redshift (left) and mass (right) kernels for the full-sky tSZ power spectrum at $\ell=500$ (blue), the masked tSZ power spectrum at $\ell=500$ with $q_{\rm cat}=5$ (orange), and the cluster number counts at $q>5$ (green). All kernels are normalised to unity over the corresponding logarithmic variable. Masking shifts the tSZ power spectrum toward lower-mass, slightly higher-redshift halos, while CNC is sharply localised near $10^{15}\,M_\odot/h$.}
173:    \label{fig:kernels_all_observables}
178:    \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{../../tsz_cnc_paper_plots/kernel_analysis/kernels_per_ell.pdf}
179:    \caption{Kernels for the three probes at multipoles $\ell\in\{50,100,500,1000\}$. Increasing $\ell$ shifts the tSZ-PS kernels toward lower mass and higher redshift, while the CNC kernel is by construction $\ell$-independent.}
180:    \label{fig:kernels_per_ell}
183:Figure~\ref{fig:kernels_all_observables} shows the three kernels at $\ell=500$, computed by integrating over $M\in[5\times10^{13},10^{16}]\,M_\odot/h$ and $z\in[0.005,3.0]$. Two lessons are immediate. First, the masked power spectrum draws its signal from a substantially lower-mass population than the full-sky power spectrum: at $\ell=500$, the mass kernel of $C_\ell^{\rm unres}$ peaks near $3\times10^{14}\,M_\odot/h$, while the full-sky kernel peaks near $6\times10^{14}\,M_\odot/h$. Second, the CNC kernel is sharply localised near $10^{15}\,M_\odot/h$, consistent with only the most massive, pressure-dominated clusters crossing the $q_{\rm cat}=5$ threshold. The three probes sample the halo population in complementary windows, and this separation is preserved bin-by-bin (Fig.~\ref{fig:kernels_per_ell}).
187:    \includegraphics[width=0.49\linewidth]{../../tsz_cnc_paper_plots/kernel_analysis/mean_mass_vs_ell.pdf}\hfill
188:    \includegraphics[width=0.49\linewidth]{../../tsz_cnc_paper_plots/kernel_analysis/mean_redshift_vs_ell.pdf}
193:Figure~\ref{fig:mean_mass_redshift_vs_ell} summarises the $\ell$-dependence of the mean values. As $\ell$ increases, the full-sky tSZ power spectrum rapidly loses sensitivity to the highest-mass halos: $\langle M\rangle$ drops from $6.9\times10^{14}\,M_\odot/h$ at $\ell=10$ to $4.0\times10^{14}\,M_\odot/h$ by $\ell\sim10^3$, and $\langle z\rangle$ rises from $0.03$ to $0.34$. The masked power spectrum is much flatter in $\ell$: for $q_{\rm cat}=5$, $\langle M\rangle$ stays within $(2.0$--$2.3)\times10^{14}\,M_\odot/h$ and $\langle z\rangle$ within $0.22$--$0.42$. The CNC kernel provides a horizontal reference: at $q>5$, $\langle M\rangle_{\rm CNC}\approx 5.5\times10^{14}\,M_\odot/h$ and $\langle z\rangle_{\rm CNC}\approx0.23$. The CNC and tSZ-PS populations overlap strongly at low $\ell$ in the unmasked case and are cleanly separated once the $q_{\rm cat}=5$ mask is applied. This is the physical reason why masking is not merely a book-keeping device but a genuine reduction of the cross-probe covariance.
680:A kernel analysis (Sec.~\ref{sec:kernels}) demonstrates that masking redistributes the information content of the tSZ power spectrum: at $\ell=500$ the unresolved population peaks at $3\times10^{14}\,M_\odot/h$, while the full-sky population peaks at $6\times10^{14}\,M_\odot/h$, and CNC samples the high-mass tail at $5.5\times10^{14}\,M_\odot/h$. The two probes therefore act on disjoint halo populations after masking, which is the physical origin of the joint-analysis gain.
---
